--- body ---
## Context

`skills/external-peer-review/peer_review.py:87` reads `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` from
`os.environ`, falling back to walking parent directories for a `.env` that
assigns it. Both paths depend on the credential being ambient or on a file that
is not the keystore.

Child of 0942. The pattern to copy is `skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh`
(ticket 0393): locate the provider file defining the variable, extract that one
variable in an `env -i` subshell, never print / log / argv the value.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Resolves from `~/.config/keys/` at point of use; env still wins if set
- [ ] The `.env`-walking fallback is removed, not merely deprioritized
- [ ] Fails loud and named when the credential cannot be resolved -- never a
      silent no-op (see feedback_gaze_external_seats_fail_open)
- [ ] Fake-sentinel test, never a real key; asserts against a child process
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine pass 2026-09-16 — premise gap, and the author's decision

**The ticket as filed would have produced a regression.** `~/.config/keys/openrouter.env`
holds NO bare `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`. It defines only `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_AEDIST`,
`_CLIMATEFINANCE`, `_IDH`, `_PI` and five `EXPIRED_*`. The canonical name exists
at runtime only because the harness `.env` `KEYS=` line RENAMES
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH` to it — i.e. it is manufactured by the very mechanism
0945 deletes. A faithful port scanning the keystore for `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`
finds nothing and fails on every invocation.

`reviewers.sh` never meets this because each seat's `panel.yml` `credential-env:`
names its keyset variant directly. `peer_review.py` had no equivalent input.

**Author's decision, 2026-09-16:** add a `--credential-env NAME` flag defaulting
to `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH`. The identity becomes an input of the caller rather
than a constant, mirroring what `panel.yml` already does per seat, so a project
invoking this skill can bill its own identity without editing code. Rejected:
hardcoding `_IDH` (a project would silently bill the harness identity), and
adding a bare `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` to the keystore (a sixth credential copy in a
file whose header promises there is no second copy).

**Shape.** Do not port `env -i` literally: Python's `subprocess.run(env=...)`
replaces the child environment natively, and there is no `BASH_ENV` re-entry to
defeat from Python. Keep a `bash -c` shell-out for the EXTRACTION itself, so the
provider file is sourced as trusted shell code with the same semantics as
`bash-env.sh` and `reviewers.sh` — a hand-rolled Python parse of the same file
would silently diverge. Locating the file needs no subprocess. The provider is
static (`openrouter.env`), so no multi-file scan is needed; the failure message
must name the exact file and variable probed.

**In scope, found by the pass and easy to miss:** `--repo-root` exists solely to
seed the `.env` walk and becomes dead with it; `skills/external-peer-review/SKILL.md:26`
documents the fallback and becomes false. Both must be swept in this change.
Nothing tested exercises the walk (`tests/test_external_peer_review_skill.py:63`
only asserts a substring appears in the source), so removal breaks no test —
which also means no test currently protects this behaviour either way.

**Antipattern scan:** clean. 0944 turns out not to be a Python consumer, so
0943 is the only one; a shared `resolver.py` module would be premature
abstraction for a single call site. Private functions in `peer_review.py`, which
is where `reviewers.sh` keeps its equivalents.

## Exit criteria (revised 2026-09-16)
- [ ] `--credential-env NAME` flag added, defaulting to `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH`
- [ ] Resolves that name from `~/.config/keys/openrouter.env` at point of use;
      an already-set environment variable still wins
- [ ] Extraction shells out so the provider file is sourced as trusted shell
      code, never hand-parsed; the value reaches neither argv nor any log
- [ ] The `.env`-walking fallback is removed, not deprioritized
- [ ] `--repo-root` removed with it, since the walk was its only consumer
- [ ] `SKILL.md:26` rewritten to describe keystore resolution
- [ ] Fails loud, naming the exact file and variable probed -- never a silent
      no-op (see feedback_gaze_external_seats_fail_open)
- [ ] Fake-sentinel test, never a real key; asserts against a child process
- [ ] `make check` passes
